INT. CHURCH BASEMENT - AA MEETING - NIGHT

Folding chairs in a circle. Burnt coffee. A banner: "ONE DAY AT A TIME."

Empty chairs, all of them. Except one.

THE WOMAN, 40s, cardigan, big purse on her lap. She waves at nobody.

THE WOMAN
Hi everybody!

(beat for the audience)

She sits up. Beams. Waits for the applause that doesn't come.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
Wow. Tough room.

Silence. A fluorescent buzz.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
So. Hi. I'm... let's say I'm new here. Three days sober. Well, "sober." Dry. Technically damp.

She laughs at herself. Nobody joins.

Her purse. It wiggles.

She pats it. Smiles wider.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
That's just my sponsor. She's shy.

Nothing. Another wiggle. A faint croak.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
Look, I know how this sounds. I had a little problem. Nothing major! A few wishes. A few... frogs.

She opens the purse. Two bulging eyes peer out.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
Kim is the one on the left. Kip is the one on the right. Honestly, I never could tell which is which. My ex-husbands. They had names for a reason.

The door at the top of the stairs creaks open. Footsteps. Heavy. The sound of a staff tapping the steps.

She does not look up. Her smile does not move.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
(whispers to the purse)
My name is Brenda. And I have been a witch for four hundred years.

The staff stops. Right behind her.

She will not turn around.

Rain on the high windows.
